the Distance Between Two Points: Crossing the Axis"} :step/id #uuid "3a335606-3bdb-5535-8956-9a3ababd7eb1" :step/math-academy-id 8892} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074901 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074978 :tutorial/content "The distance between two points in the coordinate plane is the number of units between them. Let's explore two methods for calculating the horizontal distance between two points with the points $(1,1)$ and $(5,1)$.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFirst, we plot the given points in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/19/196c50295e8d8a95084c31226fb9319ede7b70603d894b6939e4615df583c29c.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment is $4$ units, so the distance equals $4$.\n\n![](images/4b/4b531e29616f3e8d7ea0c65761e59cfea224b9c0e756c68b7647c7271dc09493.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $y$-axis and then subtracting.\n\n![](images/6d/6d5c6760e15098dc381e639cd806b6410f2fa6d9049d11d5467427635c9872b6.png)\n\nThe point $({\\color{blue}{1}},1)$ is at a distance of $\\color{blue}{1}$ from the $y$-axis, and the point $({\\color{red}{5}},1)$ is at a distance of $\\color{red}{5}$ from the $y$-axis. Therefore, the distance between the two points is\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{5}} - {\\color{blue}{1}} = 4\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "aeee8d0d-20f6-5700-8c35-c66e0eef8daa" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2199 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1961bdcc-cea7-5d78-88c1-4f69e1dab833" :step/math-academy-id 8895 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074898}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074902 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074979 :tutorial/content "When we want to find the distance between two points in different quadrants, method 1 works exactly the same, but method 2 is slightly different.\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $y$-axis for a horizontal distance, or $x$-axis for a vertical distance, and then *adding*.\n\nSo, what's the distance between $(-3,2)$ and $(1,2)$?\n\n**Method 1**\n\nWe plot the given points in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/e0/e0fec8fca43b421b62efc3d6d892563c80beaab3c2816be47aa7563868555268.png)\n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment is $4$ units, so the distance equals $4$.\n\n![](images/dd/ddc1e6cec323fe245ff2a115a587e773d408dc0115ba5002c02ac1d238620c63.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $y$-axis and then adding.\n\n![](images/0b/0bbb91fc8c0e7ad577261dcdbc8e7aaa16a44e8be2e937e2d6870aae58af4ccf.png)\n\nThe point $({\\color{blue}{1}},2)$ is at a distance of $\\color{blue}{1}$ from the $y$-axis, and the point $({\\color{red}{-3}},2)$ is at a distance of $\\color{red}{3}$ from the $y$-axis. Therefore, the distance between the two points is\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{3}} + {\\color{blue}{1}} = 4\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a1233efd-87e1-5707-9b70-24c24ed47418" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2204 :tutorial/title "The Distance Between Two Points in Different Quadrants"} :step/id #uuid "d97c5e30-e125-5cba-9973-1e1898eca20e" :step/math-academy-id 8947 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074900}}] :activity/title "Distances Between Points in the Coordinate Plane" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073991}]}
